Reduce allocations and CPU when committing and loading `DistributedSession`.

## Description

**Motivation:** every request that modifies session state calls `DistributedSession.CommitAsync`. It serializes into a growable `MemoryStream`, writing one byte at a time for the length prefixes, and then copies the result with `ToArray()`. For a session holding a few KB, that allocates the payload about three times: the doubling buffers plus the final copy. Loading wraps the cached `byte[]` in a `MemoryStream` and reads every length prefix byte by byte through virtual `Stream.ReadByte` calls.

**Change** (internal implementation only, no public API or behavior change):
- `Serialize` computes the exact payload size first, then writes into a single right-sized `byte[]` with span copies and `BinaryPrimitives`.
  - Validation is unchanged and runs before anything is written: the 3-byte entry count, then the 2-byte key length. The same `ArgumentOutOfRangeException`s are thrown.
- `Deserialize` reads straight from the cached `byte[]` through a small private struct reader that mirrors `MemoryStream` semantics:
  - `ReadByte` returns `-1` past the end.
  - `ReadBytes` allocates first, so negative lengths still throw `OverflowException`, then throws `EndOfStreamException` when data is short.
  - So malformed or truncated payloads fail exactly as before.
- **The wire format is byte-identical.**

### Benchmarks

Local BenchmarkDotNet run in-process, 3 rounds, main vs PR. The setup is `DistributedSession` with an in-memory `IDistributedCache` stub. `Commit` creates a session, `Set`s N entries and calls `CommitAsync`. `Load` creates a session, calls `LoadAsync` and does one `TryGetValue`. Means are shown as round 1 / round 2 / round 3.

| Method | Entries | Value size | main mean (ns) | PR mean (ns) | main alloc | PR alloc |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Commit | 3 | 64 B | 1,217 / 799 / 819 | 934 / 624 / 630 | 3,000 B | **2,112 B** (−30%) |
| Commit | 3 | 1 KB | 2,989 / 2,048 / 1,871 | 1,946 / 1,272 / 1,223 | 18,617 B | **10,753 B** (−42%) |
| Commit | 20 | 64 B | 5,491 / 3,936 / 3,963 | 4,698 / 3,269 / 3,255 | 15,097 B | **11,089 B** (−27%) |
| Commit | 20 | 1 KB | 16,953 / 12,086 / 11,304 | 11,158 / 7,124 / 7,589 | 136,104 B | **68,692 B** (−50%) |
| Load | 3 | 64 B | 549 / 426 / 383 | 382 / 338 / 361 | 1,192 B | 1,128 B |
| Load | 3 | 1 KB | 601 / 579 / 568 | 523 / 552 / 571 | 4,072 B | 4,008 B |
| Load | 20 | 64 B | 1,555 / 1,583 / 1,528 | 1,378 / 1,333 / 1,427 | 5,912 B | 5,848 B |
| Load | 20 | 1 KB | 2,953 / 2,857 / 2,741 | 2,712 / 2,609 / 2,608 | 25,113 B | 25,049 B |

Round 1 used a different job configuration than rounds 2–3, so its absolute numbers run higher; compare within a round. `Commit` is roughly 20–37% faster with 27–50% less allocation. `Load` is neutral to about 10% faster, saving the `MemoryStream`. None of the cases regressed. Error was ≤ ~10% of the mean in all rounds.

<details><summary>Benchmark code (not included in the PR)</summary>

```csharp
[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class SessionBenchmark
{
    private readonly FixedCache _cache = new();
    private (string Key, byte[] Value)[] _entries;
    [Params(3, 20)] public int Entries { get; set; }
    [Params(64, 1024)] public int ValueSize { get; set; }

    [GlobalSetup]
    public void Setup()
    {
        _entries = new (string, byte[])[Entries];
        for (var i = 0; i < Entries; i++) { _entries[i] = ("SessionKey_" + i, new byte[ValueSize]); }
        var s = Create(true);
        foreach (var (k, v) in _entries) { s.Set(k, v); }
        s.CommitAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }

    private DistributedSession Create(bool isNew) =>
        new DistributedSession(_cache, "key", TimeSpan.FromMinutes(20), TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1), static () => true, NullLoggerFactory.Instance, isNew);

    [Benchmark]
    public async Task<bool> Load() { var s = Create(false); await s.LoadAsync(); return s.TryGetValue("SessionKey_0", out _); }

    [Benchmark]
    public async Task Commit()
    {
        var s = Create(true);
        foreach (var (k, v) in _entries) { s.Set(k, v); }
        await s.CommitAsync();
    }
    // FixedCache: IDistributedCache that stores the last Set value and returns it from Get/GetAsync.
}
```
</details>

### Tests
- New tests in `SessionTests`:
  - `DistributedSession_CommitWritesExpectedFormat`: an exact byte-for-byte golden payload, including a multi-byte UTF-8 key and an empty value.
  - `DistributedSession_RoundTripsValues`: 100 KB value, empty value, 1,000-char key.
  - `DistributedSession_TruncatedPayload_LoadAsyncThrowsEndOfStream`: `LoadAsync` still throws `EndOfStreamException`, and the sync path still marks the session unavailable.
  - `DistributedSession_UnknownRevision_IsTreatedAsEmptyAndRewritten`.
- All 4 new tests **also pass against the `main` implementation**, which confirms identical format and failure behavior.
- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Session.Tests`: 32/32 passed.

